[{:db/id 17592186179959 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{5}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise. Interpret circulation as the sum of four convergent improper line integrals over the open quadrant arcs, with independent one-sided limits at $(\\pm1,0)$ and $(0,\\pm5)$. The field is undefined at those four points, so the original ordinary circulation is not defined." :question/worked-solution "The original field is undefined at the four axis intersections, so an ordinary line integral over the whole ellipse is not defined. For the explicit improper interpretation, $r(t)=(\\cos t,5\\sin t)$ traverses $25x^2+y^2=25$ counterclockwise. On each open quadrant arc,\n\n$$\\frac{5}{y}\\,dx=\\frac{5}{5\\sin t}(-\\sin t)\\,dt=-dt,\\qquad\\frac{1}{x}\\,dy=\\frac{1}{\\cos t}(5\\cos t)\\,dt=5dt.$$\n\nThus the scalar pullback is $4$. Each endpoint has the finite one-sided limit $4$, so each open-arc integral converges absolutely and equals $2\\pi$. Their sum is $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{imp}}=8\\pi$, the unchanged option c. No principal-value cancellation is used, and $F$ remains undefined at the excluded points." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
